d-solution" "value" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{3\\pi /2}^{7\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/state.json" "file_sha256" "9791982ed0c3cfac3cf7daeacafed38bd81f26e5b387b69d7cbe790d92d19534"} {"question" "q-103842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "126a5613e9d54d7b3f561384301afea7c550865541449bfed6cdb2fdc8291a97"} {"question" "q-103842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/state.json" "file_sha256" "9791982ed0c3cfac3cf7daeacafed38bd81f26e5b387b69d7cbe790d92d19534"} {"question" "q-103842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{16}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{8}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/state.json" "file_sha256" "9791982ed0c3cfac3cf7daeacafed38bd81f26e5b387b69d7cbe790d92d19534"} {"question" "q-103842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/state.json" "file_sha256" "9791982ed0c3cfac3cf7daeacafed38bd81f26e5b387b69d7cbe790d92d19534"} {"question" "e-7400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/e5647c0f19727d1e626e637464cc57444e0bc0f2f8e99a4afd15d3c3631dc3f0.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the arc length of the polar curve $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi,$ shown in blue in the diagram above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/example-7400.json" "file_sha256" "28619c4d4743f4c652ae2549f40de343a6bc6f724b13059b8072ffa6dbc2bf98"} {"question" "e-7400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} =-7\\cos(7\\theta)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ))^{2}+(-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+49{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+1+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/example-7400.json" "file_sha256" "28619c4d4743f4c652ae2549f40de343a6bc6f724b13059b8072ffa6dbc2bf98"} {"question" "e-641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/0bb9e5e7315d4bcd5fc21700fdd2597f6d9b585a109145de53c6f94e9530eabf.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ (shown in blue in the picture above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/example-641.json" "file_sha256" "d0167d64c4c6e7ff59561f7df2ef5270c1a704998c8d2f2f9140715d7d16fdff"} {"question" "e-641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now use the half-angle formula $1-\\cos\\theta =2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={-4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/example-641.json" "file_sha256" "d0167d64c4c6e7ff59561f7df2ef5270c1a704998c8d2f2f9140715d7d16fdff"}] "usage" {"e-641" [] "q-9532" [] "e-7400" []} "basis" 2232 "retractions" [[17592186092869 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/999/Source/Images/q-9532.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r = 2\\sin θ$ for $\\frac{π}{2} \\le θ \\le \\frac{5π}{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)."] [17592186092070 :question/problem "Find an integral expression for the arc length of the polar curve $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$, shown in blue in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/999/Source/Images/9a98033ef7124397b87e336d2d7778e9.png)"] [17592186092070 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr=3-\\sin(7\\theta)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} =-7\\cos(7\\theta)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(3 - \\sin (7θ))^{2} + (-7\\cos (7θ))^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{9 - 6\\sin (7θ) + \\sin^{2} (7θ) + 49\\cos^{2} (7θ)}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{9 - 6\\sin (7θ) + \\sin^{2} (7θ) + \\cos^{2} (7θ) + 48\\cos^{2} (7θ)}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{9 - 6\\sin (7θ) + 1 + 48\\cos^{2} (7θ)}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{10 - 6\\sin (7θ) + 48\\cos^{2} (7θ)}dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186092040 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ (shown in blue in the picture above).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/999/Source/Images/7b8c89d9e599587ac6274b08d6202514.png)"] [17592186092040 :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr=2(1-\\cos\\theta)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(2 - 2\\cos θ)^{2} + (2\\sin θ)^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{4 - 8\\cos θ + 4\\cos^{2} θ + 4\\sin^{2} θ}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{4 - 8\\cos θ + 4}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{8 - 8\\cos θ}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{1 - \\cos θ}dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now use the half-angle formula\n\n$$\n1-\\cos\\theta =2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nto give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= 2\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{2\\sin^{2} (\\frac{θ}{2})}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot ∫_{0}^{π}\\sin (\\frac{θ}{2})dθ \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot 2(\\cos (\\frac{θ}{2})) \\mid _{0}^{π} \\\\\n&=-8(\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) - \\cos 0) \\\\\n&=-8(0 - 1) \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186091680 17592186091681 17592186091682 17592186091683 17592186091684] "immutable_fields" [[17592186091679 :answer-field/correct] [17592186091679 :answer-field/id] [17592186091679 :answer-field/key] [17592186091679 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
